# Cloudflare Pages response headers (static assets only -- a Pages Function
# sets its own headers and these rules never reach it).
#
# The problem this fixes: without explicit caching rules, an HTML page (e.g.
# chart.html) could be served stale from the browser/edge cache — so a freshly
# deployed page that references a new ?v= asset is never fetched, and chart
# updates appear "not to ship". HTML, scan data and sw.js are therefore always
# revalidated, while versioned assets are cached hard.
#
# HOW PAGES COMBINES RULES (REFINE200 #75, 2026-10-14). Every rule whose path
# matches applies, in file order, and a header two rules both set is JOINED
# with a comma -- a more specific path does NOT win. The /js/* rule below used
# to read "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, public, max-age=86400" on the
# wire, and caches take the first max-age, so the scripts were never cached at
# all. A rule that replaces a header first DETACHES the inherited one with
# "! Cache-Control" (Pages applies a rule's detaches before its own values).
# Pinned by test/site_headers.test.js.

# Everything: revalidate by default so the latest deploy is always served.
# The three security headers (REFINE200 #56) apply to every page: only this
# site may frame it (frame-ancestors, plus X-Frame-Options for old browsers),
# and a link out sends the origin, never the full URL with its query string.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# Versioned assets: cached for a year and never revalidated. Safe ONLY because
# every page asks for /js/ and /css/ with a ?v= that changes on every edit
# (CLAUDE.md rule 2; test/cache.test.js pins one version per asset across
# pages, test/site_headers.test.js pins that every reference carries one), the
# vendor bundle carries its version in its file name, and a font is never
# edited in place (a new font is a new file name). The service worker already
# treats ?v= assets as cache-first, so this extends the same rule to the HTTP
# cache. Forget a ?v= bump and a returning visitor keeps the old file.
/js/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/css/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/vendor/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/fonts/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Scan output JSON is rewritten every scan — must always be fresh. /* already
# says so; this keeps the intent explicit (detached first, so it is not joined
# into a doubled value).
/data/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
